      "rls_enabled_no_policy": "Five 038 service-only tables (attendance_mutation_receipts, device_tokens, result_slip_upload_intents, security_principals, student_storage_cleanup_queue) enable RLS, revoke table grants from anon/authenticated, and have zero policies so only BYPASSRLS (service_role) can read/write. Same pattern as accepted rate_limit_events. Adding a client policy would undo 038; a USING(false) policy would not change access and would add production drift. device_tokens previously had 'owner manages own'; 038 dropped that policy and routes writes through register_device_token.",
      "authenticated_security_definer_function_executable": "31 functions are client RPCs or RLS/storage helper predicates. Anon EXECUTE is revoked; authenticated EXECUTE is required (clients call the RPCs; RLS/storage policies must be able to execute the helpers). Each client RPC self-guards with role/feature/ownership checks; helpers are auth.uid()-scoped booleans. This matches the settled is_admin()/is_parent()/... WARN. is_superadmin() is used by web RPC and feature-flag RLS. apply_attendance_clear / attendance_mutation_is_replay are internal helpers that remain self-guarded; hiding them from PostgREST would be a later grant-tightening migration, not a current hole.",
